WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.770
There are a few topics that are considered
off-limits at my family’s Thanksgiving table,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.880
and one of them is TAXES, or more specifically,
the American tax SYSTEM.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.640
Virtually everyone has a strong opinion about
it: It’s unfair!

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:14.860
It doesn’t go far enough!

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.289
It’s too complicated!

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:21.080
Cable news and social media are filled with
passionate debates about it, but how many

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.100
people actually know how our current tax system
works?

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.940
If your eyes glaze over when you hear phrases
like “progressive taxation” and “marginal

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.440
brackets,” you’re not alone.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.800
Even some of our law-makers seem to have a
shaky grip on the concept.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.430
But if you hope to have a say in a system
that impacts your wallet every day, you should

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.860
understand how—and why—it ended up this
way.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.239
Let’s start with something all of us can
agree on.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.020
The U.S. tax code is complicated.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.200
Really complicated.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.480
It’s one of the most complicated systems
in the world, taking the average citizen 13

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.760
hours to file each year.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.340
Compare that to, say, Japan, where it’s
closer to 15 minutes.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.939
And one reason we have such a confusing system
is because our income is taxed at different

00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:18.740
rates, depending on how much we earn.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.869
For nearly 100 years, our country had no income
tax.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:29.110
The first one was levied in 1861 during the
civil war at a flat 3% tax on income over

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:36.680
$800.The next year, in 1862, the first “progressive
tax bracket” appeared, with the 3% rate

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.840
AND a 5% rate for any income earned over $10,000.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:47.240
Then in 1872, the income tax was repealed
because — get this — the government no

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.220
longer needed the money.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.340
Can you even imagine…?

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.940
Then in 1913 with the 16th Amendment, progressive
taxes made a permanent come-back.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.100
It gave the Federal government the free reign
to tax workers’ income in every state.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.360
So what is a “progressive tax”?

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.060
Well, you can think of tax systems coming
in one of three main flavors.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:11.560
Regressive, Proportional, or Progressive.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.200
A regressive tax impacts lower paid workers
more severely.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.300
Paying sales tax on groceries or clothing
will end up being a higher portion of a low-earner’s

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.940
pay check than a high-earner.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:30.200
A worker earning $20,000 a year who pays $1,000
in sales tax will be parting with 5% of his income.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:36.260
Another making $100,000 paying that same tax
for the same goods will only be charged 1%

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.540
of her income for the same thing.

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:43.590
A simpler solution would be a proportional
tax, where, simply put, everybody pays the

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.890
same percentage of their income, no matter
how much they make.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.920
Nine states currently impose a proportional,

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.500
flat state income tax.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.620
That means no matter how much you make, your
tax rate stays the same.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.660
For example, 5% of every dollar earned.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:04.099
Finally there’s the progressive tax, a system
that has a heavier impact on high earners.

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:08.840
Low earners may end up paying little or nothing,
with only the wealthiest people paying the

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:09.989
highest tax rates.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.590
How does this actually work?

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:16.440
Through a series of different tax levels or
“marginal tax brackets”.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.530
Buckle up, it’s about to get exciting!

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.900
Let’s say you’re a single person making
$60,000/yr.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.700
A quick look at the IRS Tax Brackets will
tell you that you fall into the 22% bracket.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.200
Do you have to give the government 22% of
your income?

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.140
No!

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.660
A common misconception is that entering a
higher tax bracket means your whole income

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.440
is taxed at the higher rate.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.739
So how much are you actually paying?

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:47.420
After taking your available deductions, you’re
left with $47,800 in “Taxable income”.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:53.769
As of 2019 we have 7 marginal tax brackets
between 10 and 37%.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:59.520
Each bracket along the way applies to one
specific chunk of your income, not the whole thing.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:06.660
Bracket 1 is for your first $9,525, and it
gets taxed at 10%.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.340
Next stop is Bracket 2.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:14.930
This applies to $29,175, and is taxed at a
rate of 12%.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:22.020
Last stop is Bracket 3, where your remaining
income of $9,100 will be taxed at 22%.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:28.180
Even though you’re in the 22% tax bracket,
your “effective tax rate” is only 10%,

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.940
thanks to the marginal rates in our progressive
tax system.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:38.760
So a single-filer with a taxable income of
$500,001 may be in the highest tax-bracket

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:42.950
of 37%, but only $1.00 is taxed at that rate.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.470
The effective rate will be much lower.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.960
This system is definitely more complicated
than a flat-tax.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.540
But is it fair?

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.840
Simply put; no.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.110
A progressive tax system, by definition is
unfair.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.420
It allows lower earners to pay very little
tax…

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.640
In fact more than 44% of Americans pay no
federal income taxes.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.480
And the more money you make, the less of it
you will keep.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.900
Opponents of progressive taxation tend to
be higher earners, arguing that the system

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:17.160
“punishes” wealthy people, and actively
discourages making a higher income.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.620
They claim that a flat-tax would be simpler,
easier, and more fair.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.880
Supporters of a progressive tax, however,
point to the real-world human impact on struggling

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.080
families and low wage earners.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.120
They argue that if people have a greater ability
to pay, then they should pay more.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.160
And a progressive tax system, they claim,
can counteract the regressive nature of sales

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.800
taxes, easing the impact of taxes on your
rent or groceries, while hitting you harder

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.300
in your ability to take vacations or buy luxury
goods.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:52.060
And while nobody loves paying taxes, the average
US tax-payer actually pays less than almost

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.620
any other developed nation.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.630
Compared to, say Belgium, we get off pretty
easy.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:05.080
Their lowest tax-bracket is 25%, and the highest
is 50% for anything over 38,080 Euros!

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.630
Do you feel our progressive tax-system encourages
laziness?

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:11.440
Does it discourage people from earning more
money?

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.280
Or is it giving a leg-up to people with lower
salaries?

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.960
Whatever your opinion, now that you understand
the mechanics of marginal brackets and progressive

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:25.620
taxation, you’ll be in a much better place
to argue about it over your next Thanksgiving dinner.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.580
Or in the comments section.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:30.300
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.420
